The fix is `x.insert(*exons.get(0).unwrap());`. Read it from the inside out. `exons` is a slice of tuples, so `exons.get(0)` looks up the first element and returns `Option<&(u64, u64)>`. The `&` is the problem. `HashSet::insert` wants a tuple, and `get` hands you a reference to one. Put `*` in front and it dereferences the tuple, and `unwrap()` pulls the value out of the `Option`.

The error messages point at the same mistake from two sides. The first says the argument to `get` should be a reference, which is true but not the problem. The second says `insert` got an `Option` when it wanted a tuple, and that one is closer to the root.

There is a bug under the fix, and it shows when a transcript has no exons. Then `get(0)` returns `None`, `unwrap()` panics, and the transcript is lost with it. A panic in a loop over a whole annotation file is a bad way to find missing data. Change it to `if let Some(&first) = exons.first() { x.insert(first); }`. That skips the empty ones and leaves them in the output, which you can check later. If you would rather count them, keep a counter and report it at the end.

The loop has a second bug, and it is worse than the first. `any` stops at the first match, so this transcript joins the first group it overlaps and never looks at the rest. Then the groups stop being groups of overlapping transcripts and become groups of transcripts that happened to land in the same pass. Run it on two transcripts that overlap each other but come in at different positions in the list and they end up in different groups.

