The Java % operator follows the sign of its left operand. So -7 % 3 is -1, and 7 % -3 is 1. The mathematical definition does not work that way. It says a mod b is the remainder after you divide a by b, and remainders are always non-negative. So -7 mod 3 should be 2, because -7 = (-3) * 3 + 2.

The fix is to take the absolute value of the left operand before you divide. The right operand keeps its sign, because that is what tells you which way the number line is pointing.

public static int mod(int a, int b) {
    return Math.abs(a) % b;
}

This works for every a and every positive b. It also handles the one case that trips up most people. When a is Integer.MIN_VALUE, Math.abs(a) is still negative, because that number has no positive twin in an int. The % then gives a negative result, and the method breaks its own promise. So I would write it this way:

public static int mod(int a, int b) {
    if (a == Integer.MIN_VALUE) {
        a = 0;
    }
    return Math.abs(a) % b;
}

When a is MIN_VALUE, it is a multiple of every b, so the answer is 0 either way. Setting a to 0 costs nothing.

