The C# snippet is a good start, but it does one thing I would change. When you hold left or right while going up or down, it tilts the whole input, so holding right while climbing sends you up and to the right. Most platformers let you move sideways while you jump, and this one should too. The fix is to read the horizontal input twice, once on its own and once tilted, and to use the plain one whenever you are not climbing. Here is the whole function with that change in, written in the same style as your code:

```csharp
private Vector3 GetInputDirectionWithAngles()
{
    float horizontal = Input.GetActionStrength("ui_right") - Input.GetActionStrength("ui_left");
    float vertical = Input.GetActionStrength("ui_down") - Input.GetActionStrength("ui_up");

    Vector3 inputDirection;

    if (Math.Abs(vertical) > Mathf.Epsilon)
    {
        float tiltAngle = (vertical > 0) ? UpwardAngleDeg : DownwardAngleDeg;
        float rise = Mathf.Tan(Mathf.Deg2Rad(tiltAngle));
        inputDirection = new Vector3(horizontal, rise, 1).Normalized();
    }
    else
    {
        inputDirection = new Vector3(horizontal, 0, 1).Normalized();
    }

    // Allow left and right while climbing, without tilting the climb.
    if (Math.Abs(horizontal) > Mathf.Epsilon && Math.Abs(vertical) > Mathf.Epsilon)
    {
        inputDirection.x += horizontal;
        inputDirection = inputDirection.Normalized();
    }

    return inputDirection;
}
```

The first branch is unchanged, so climbing still tilts. The new block adds plain horizontal movement to a climb, and normalizes again so the speed stays constant in every direction. Try it and see whether it feels right. Some games tilt on a jump and keep the old direction while you hold a side. That is a matter of taste, and this is only one way to do it.

