The Java % operator is remainder, not modulo. It follows the sign of the dividend, so -7 % 3 is -1. True modulo follows the sign of the divisor and is always non-negative when b > 0. For negative a, add b to the remainder:

public static int mod(int a, int b) {
    int r = a % b;
    return (r < 0) ? r + b : r;
}

When a is negative, a = k * b + r with r negative. Adding b gives a = (k + 1) * b + (r + b), and r + b is the non-negative residue. When a is non-negative, r already satisfies 0 <= r < b.

If you want to avoid % entirely, use repeated subtraction for non-negative a:

public static int mod(int a, int b) {
    if (a < 0) {
        a = -a;
        a = b - (a % b);
        if (a == b) {
            a = 0;
        }
    }
    while (a >= b) {
        a -= b;
    }
    return a;
}

This version is slower for large a, since it subtracts b one step at a time.

