CHAMELEON changes what mobs do by rewriting their AI at runtime. You write an extension, tell it which mob and which behaviour you want, and it swaps that behaviour out for your own. The mob keeps its looks and its loot, and only its mind changes.

Here is the basic shape of an extension:

```java
import net.creeperhost.chameleon.api.ModExtension;
import net.creeperhost.chameleon.api.data.ModData;
import net.creeperhost.chameleon.api.event.ModEvent;
import net.creeperhost.chameleon.api.mob.MobWrapper;
import net.minecraft.world.entity.Mob;
import net.minecraft.world.entity.ai.behavior.Behavior;

public class MyExtension extends ModExtension {

  @Override
  public void onInitialize(ModData data) {
    // Called when the mod loads. Set up your events here.
  }

  @ModEvent(mob = "minecraft:creeper")
  public void makeCreeperFriendly(MobWrapper<Mob> wrapper) {
    // This runs every time a Creeper is created.
    // Replace its AI with something gentler.
  }
}
```

The `@ModEvent` annotation is where the magic happens. Its `mob` value is the resource location of the mob you want to change. Every time that mob spawns, Chameleon calls your method and hands you a wrapper around it. Inside, you can change its goal selector, its behaviour, or anything else the mob exposes.

For example, to stop Creepers from exploding, set their goal selector to an empty one:

```java
@ModEvent(mob = "minecraft:creeper")
public void makeCreeperFriendly(MobWrapper<Mob> wrapper) {
  Mob mob = wrapper.getEntity();
  if (mob instanceof net.minecraft.world.entity.monster.Creeper creeper) {
    creeper.setTargeting(net.minecraft.world.entity.ai.targeting.Targeting.EMPTY);
  }
}
```

The same pattern works for any mob. Change the `mob` value and adapt the cast. If you want every zombie to walk toward the nearest player instead of chasing anyone who sees it, write a behaviour that finds the closest player and sets it as the zombie's target, then plug that in where the original behaviour was.

A few things are worth knowing. The first is that Chameleon only touches what it can reach through reflection. It cannot rewrite code that has already run, so changes that happen during a mob's construction may not take effect. The second is that if two mods edit the same mob, the one that loads last wins. The third is that you cannot change a mob's model or texture through Chameleon. For that you need a resource pack.

To get started, add the Chameleon API to your build. The exact instruction depends on your loader, but it is a dependency on `net.creeperhost:chameleon-api`. Then put your extension class on the classpath with a `chameleon.json` file that names it. When the game starts, Chameleon reads that file and runs your code.

